    {
      "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/fhir-harness/process/Process_IgRelease",
      "@type": "https://litlfred.github.io/bootstrap/v0/ns/Process",
      "name": "An IG release: the owner tags, a draft is built, the owner publishes",
      "summary": "How an IG repository's release is cut, and how a missed one is caught up (skill `ig-binary-artefacts` §\"Releasing\").",
      "description": "How an IG repository's release is cut, and how a missed one is caught up (skill `ig-binary-artefacts` §\"Releasing\"). (Owner, 2026-11-10: \"need to add this to process (and how to catchup if not tagged) of build\".)\n\nTHE RULE. A release is a governance decision. The owner tags the IG's CURRENT version from `sushi-config.yaml`, `v<version>`, on the default branch. The tag starts the IG's `ig-release.yml` caller, which builds the tag in full and makes a GitHub DRAFT release carrying `package.tgz` (and its R4/R4B variants), `ast-data.zip` and `SHA256SUMS`. The owner publishes the draft. An agent never chooses a version, never pushes a tag and never publishes a release.\n\nTHE CATCH-UP. Every AST refresh of the default branch runs `ig-release.ts check` (advisory, never failing the run): an untagged current version is reported with the commit that set it, for the owner to tag; a tag with no release is reported with the by-hand run of the release workflow; a draft is reported as waiting for the owner.\n\nThe site is not the release: it is published from the AST cache (`ig-ast-cache.bpmn`) and carries none of these files.",
      "enforcement": "advisory",
